.TH "micropython_unix_shell" "1" "2026-06-09" "1.0" "MicroPython Unix Shell Manual"
.SH NAME
micropython_unix_shell \- A lightweight Unix-like shell for MicroPython systems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shell.run(username)
.SH DESCRIPTION
MicroPython Unix Shell is a command-line interface providing Unix-like functionality for MicroPython systems such as Raspberry Pi Pico. It includes file system operations, user management, environment configuration, and network control.

.SH SHELL BUILT-IN COMMANDS

.TP
.B clear
Clear the terminal screen.

.TP
.B exit
Exit the shell and logout the current user.

.TP
.B env
Display all environment variables and their current values.

.SH FILE SYSTEM COMMANDS

.SS pwd(1)
.B pwd
.br
Print the current working directory path.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Returns the absolute path of the current working directory.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ pwd
/home/user
.fi

.SS cd(1)
.B cd [directory]
.br
Change the current working directory.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Navigate to the specified directory. If no directory is specified, changes to the user's home directory. Supports absolute and relative paths.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ cd /home
$ cd ..
$ cd my-directory
.fi

.SS ls(1)
.B ls [path]
.br
List directory contents.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Display files and directories in the specified path or current directory. Shows file names, types, and sizes in KB. Directories are marked with "/" and displayed in blue.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ ls
$ ls /home/user
.fi

.SS mkdir(1)
.B mkdir directory [directory ...]
.br
Create new directories.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Create one or more directories. Fails if directory already exists or if parent directory does not exist.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ mkdir newdir
$ mkdir dir1 dir2 dir3
.fi

.SS touch(1)
.B touch file [file ...]
.br
Create new empty files.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Create one or more empty files. Fails if file already exists.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ touch myfile.txt
$ touch file1.txt file2.txt
.fi

.SS type(1)
.B type file [file ...]
.br
Display file contents.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Print the contents of one or more files to the standard output. Files are displayed in chunks with file path headers.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ type myfile.txt
$ type file1.txt file2.txt
.fi

.SS cp(1)
.B cp source [source ...] destination
.br
Copy files or directories.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Copy one or more source files or directories to a destination. If destination exists as a file, prompts for action (Overwrite, Append, Cancel). When copying directories, performs recursive copy.
.TP
.B OPTIONS
.TP
(interactive)
If destination file exists, prompts for [O]verwrite, [A]ppend, or [C]ancel.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ cp file.txt /backup/
$ cp dir1 /backup/
$ cp file1.txt file2.txt /backup/
.fi

.SS mv(1)
.B mv source [source ...] destination
.br
Move or rename files and directories.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Move one or more source files or directories to a destination. Can be used to rename files. If moving multiple sources, destination must be a directory.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ mv oldname.txt newname.txt
$ mv file1.txt file2.txt /archive/
.fi

.SS rm(1)
.B rm path [path ...]
.br
Remove files and directories.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Delete one or more files or directories. For non-empty directories, prompts for confirmation before deletion. Performs recursive deletion for directories.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ rm myfile.txt
$ rm directory/
.fi

.SS du(1)
.B du [-v] path
.br
Report disk usage.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Calculate total disk space used by a file or directory in KB and bytes. The -v (verbose) flag displays usage for each file and subdirectory.
.TP
.B OPTIONS
.TP
.B -v
Verbose mode - display size for each item in path.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ du /home/user
/home/user size: 2.342 KB (2342 bytes)

$ du -v /home/user
  file1.txt 1.230 KB
  file2.txt 1.112 KB
/home/user size: 2.342 KB (2342 bytes)
.fi

.SH TEXT OUTPUT COMMANDS

.SS echo(1)
.B echo [text]
.br
Display text or redirect to file.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Print text to the console. Supports output redirection using > (write) or >> (append) operators.
.TP
.B OPERATORS
.TP
.B >
Write text to file (overwrite if exists).
.TP
.B >>
Append text to file.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ echo "Hello, World!"
$ echo "Log entry" > /tmp/log.txt
$ echo "Another entry" >> /tmp/log.txt
.fi

.SH USER MANAGEMENT COMMANDS

.SS useradd(1)
.B useradd username homedir
.br
Add a new user account.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Create a new user account with specified home directory. Only admin users can execute this command. If home directory doesn't exist, it will be created.
.TP
.B ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B username
Name of the new user account.
.TP
.B homedir
Path to user's home directory.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ useradd alice /home/alice
.fi

.SS userdel(1)
.B userdel username
.br
Delete a user account.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Remove a user account from the system. Only admin users can execute this command. Optionally removes the user's home directory.
.TP
.B ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B username
Name of the user account to delete.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ userdel alice
remove alice's home '/home/alice' [Y/n] y
.fi

.SS passwd(1)
.B passwd [username]
.br
Set or change a user's password.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Change the password for the current user or (with admin privileges) for another user. Requires old password verifitypeion. New password must be confirmed.
.TP
.B ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B username
(Optional) User whose password to change. If omitted, changes current user's password.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ passwd
[admin] passwd: ****
newpasswd> ****
confirm> ****
password updated

$ passwd alice
[alice] passwd: ****
newpasswd> ****
confirm> ****
password updated
.fi

.SS users(1)
.B users
.br
List all user accounts.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Display a list of all user accounts on the system, one per line.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ users
admin
alice
bob
.fi

.SS su(1)
.B su [username]
.br
Switch to another user account.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Start a new shell session as a different user. If no username specified, switches to admin user. Requires user's password.
.TP
.B ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B username
(Optional) User to switch to. Defaults to admin.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ su
$ su alice
.fi

.SH ENVIRONMENT COMMANDS

.SS set(1)
.B set name value
.br
Set an environment variable.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Create or modify an environment variable that persists for the current shell session. Variables can be used in subsequent commands.
.TP
.B ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B name
Variable name.
.TP
.B value
Variable value.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ set MYVAR "hello"
$ set PROMPT "[{USER}]> "
.fi

.SS unset(1)
.B unset name [name ...]
.br
Remove environment variables.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Delete one or more environment variables from the current session.
.TP
.B ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B name
Name(s) of variable(s) to remove.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ unset MYVAR
$ unset VAR1 VAR2 VAR3
.fi

.SS reset(1)
.B reset
.br
Reset environment to defaults.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Clear all custom environment variables and restore default values.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ reset
.fi

.SH NETWORK COMMANDS

.SS net(1)
.B net [action]
.br
Configure network interface.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Display or control WiFi network interface. Provides network information and connection management.
.TP
.B ACTIONS
.TP
.B (no action)
Display current network configuration and connection status.
.TP
.B up
Activate the WiFi interface.
.TP
.B down
Deactivate the WiFi interface.
.TP
.B scan
Scan for available WiFi networks.
.TP
.B connect
Connect to a WiFi network (interactive).
.TP
.B close
Disconnect from current network.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ net
inet      : 192.168.1.100
subnet    : 255.255.255.0
gateway   : 192.168.1.1
broadcast : 192.168.1.255
state: connected

$ net scan
1. HomeNetwork
2. GuestWiFi

$ net connect
---------- available networks ----------
1. HomeNetwork
2. GuestWiFi
Enter network index or [C]ancel
network index> 1
(HomeNetwork) passwd> ****
(HomeNetwork) ip: 192.168.1.100

$ net up
$ net down
$ net close
.fi

.SH SYSTEM INFORMATION COMMANDS

.SS date(1)
.B date
.br
Display current date and time.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Print the system date and time in the format: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ date
2026-06-09 14:30:45
.fi

.SS uname(1)
.B uname
.br
Display system information.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Print MicroPython version and platform information.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ uname
MicroPython x.x.x on platform
.fi

.SH DOCUMENTATION

.SS man(1)
.B man [command]
.br
Display command manual.
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
Show help information for a specific command or all available commands. Each entry displays the command name and a brief description.
.TP
.B ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B command
(Optional) Command to display help for. If omitted, shows all commands.
.TP
.B EXAMPLE
.nf
$ man
(pwd) DESCRIPTION: return present working directory
(echo) DESCRIPTION: echos the specified text
...

$ man ls
(ls) DESCRIPTION: lists the directories and files in pwd
.fi

.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES

The shell maintains several environment variables:

.TP
.B HOME
User's home directory path.
.TP
.B USER
Currently logged-in username.
.TP
.B HOST
System hostname (default: "Micro pico").
.TP
.B PWD
Current working directory.
.TP
.B LAST_ERROR
Last error message (or "success" if last command succeeded).
.TP
.B PROMPT
Custom prompt template.
.TP
.B CUSTOM VARIABLES
User-defined variables set with the
.B set
command.

.SS PROMPT FORMAT

The PROMPT variable supports the following placeholders:

.TP
.B {USER}
Current username.
.TP
.B {HOST}
System hostname.
.TP
.B {PWD}
Current working directory.
.TP
.B {DATE}
Current date (YYYY-MM-DD).
.TP
.B {TIME}
Current time (HH:MM:SS).
.TP
.B {LAST_STATUS}
Status inditypeor (✔ or ✘).

.SH EXAMPLES

.SS Create and manage directories:
.nf
$ mkdir /projects
$ cd /projects
$ mkdir python node javascript
$ ls
.fi

.SS Backup files:
.nf
$ mkdir /backup
$ cp /important/file.txt /backup/
$ cp /important/data/ /backup/
.fi

.SS Create a log file:
.nf
$ echo "System started" > /var/log.txt
$ echo "User logged in" >> /var/log.txt
$ type /var/log.txt
.fi

.SS Manage users:
.nf
$ useradd alice /home/alice
$ passwd alice
$ su alice
$ users
.fi

.SS Check disk usage:
.nf
$ du /
$ du -v /home
.fi

.SS Network operations:
.nf
$ net
$ net scan
$ net connect
.fi

.SH PERMISSIONS

.TP
.B admin
The default administrator user with full system access. Can manage users, modify files anywhere, and execute privileged operations.

.TP
.B Regular Users
Limited to their home directory and shared system areas. Cannot execute administrative commands like
.B useradd,
.B userdel,
or
.B net
configuration.

.SH SPECIAL FILES

.TP
.B /shell-config.json
User account configuration file storing username-to-home-directory mappings.

.TP
.B /passwd.{username}.bin
SHA256 hashed password file for each user account.

.SH PLATFORM SUPPORT

This shell is optimized for MicroPython systems including:
.TP
- Raspberry Pi Pico
.TP
- ESP32
.TP
- Other compatible MicroPython boards

.SH KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS

.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Send interrupt signal (SIGINT) to the shell.

.SH LIMITATIONS

.TP
- No pipe operations (|)
.TP
- No command chaining with semicolon (;)
.TP
- Limited relative path support
.TP
- Passwords stored as SHA256 binary hashes
.TP
- Single user login at a time

.SH RETURN VALUES

Commands return 0 on success and 1 on failure. The status is tracked in the LAST_ERROR environment variable and displayed in the prompt with status inditypeors:

.TP
.B ✔ (green)
Last command succeeded.
.TP
.B ✘ (red)
Last command failed.

